    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The issue is that the new tool is essentially just a glorified search engine—it checks if a citation exists, but it can't evaluate if the citation actually *supports* the claim being made in a complex argument. The committee is worried that the quality of the journal's reviews will drop significantly because we'll lose that layer of logical synthesis. How do you think the journal should handle that loss of depth?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The journal should consider implementing a hybrid approach rather than moving to a complete black-and-white solution. They could use the deterministic tool to handle foundational citation verification while reserving a limited capacity for generative review on complex argumentative structures. This allows them to leverage the new tool's reliability for basic checks while retaining some layer of sophisticated logical evaluation for high-impact reviews. It would also be wise to implement rigorous human oversight to bridge the gap between mechanical checking and nuanced synthesis.",
      "source": "target",
